6
00:00:23,727 --> 00:00:26,677
The bands are durations: homes, offices,

7
00:00:26,727 --> 00:00:31,527
partnerships, the war.
Separate by tags, choose home,

8
00:00:31,577 --> 00:00:34,737
and the places he lived
line up on their own track.

9
00:00:34,787 --> 00:00:39,497
Two Kentucky farms, thirteen
years in Indiana, New Salem,

10
00:00:39,547 --> 00:00:41,346
Springfield, and Washington.

11
00:00:42,896 --> 00:00:48,726
Zoom into the first twenty years. The
family crosses into Indiana when he's

12
00:00:48,776 --> 00:00:53,326
seven. His mother dies when he's nine,
and a year later his father brings home a

13
00:00:53,376 --> 00:00:59,496
stepmother who encourages his
reading. His sister dies in 1828,

14
00:00:59,546 --> 00:01:02,824
and that spring he takes a
flatboat to New Orleans.

15
00:01:04,364 --> 00:01:10,604
In 1831 he settles in New Salem, on
his own. After a spring as a militia

16
00:01:10,654 --> 00:01:15,704
captain, he loses his first
election. Two years later he wins,

17
00:01:15,754 --> 00:01:20,924
the first of four terms in the Illinois
House, and by 1837 he's a lawyer,

18
00:01:20,974 --> 00:01:23,319
riding into Springfield.

19
00:01:25,049 --> 00:01:30,909
Pull out one thread: family.
He marries Mary Todd in 1842,

20
00:01:30,959 --> 00:01:34,309
and they have four sons.
Two of them are bands here,

21
00:01:34,359 --> 00:01:37,339
because their whole lives
fit inside his: Eddie,

22
00:01:37,389 --> 00:01:41,169
who died before he was four,
and Willie, who died at eleven,

23
00:01:41,219 --> 00:01:44,408
in the White House, in
the war's first winter.

24
00:01:46,148 --> 00:01:50,138
After one term in Congress
he goes back to the law.

25
00:01:50,188 --> 00:01:54,198
That band, Away from
politics, runs five years,

26
00:01:54,248 --> 00:02:01,498
until the Kansas–Nebraska Act of 1854
opens the western territories to slavery.

27
00:02:01,548 --> 00:02:05,548
Follow its link: that October,
at Peoria, he answers it.

28
00:02:07,098 --> 00:02:11,328
Open a moment to read it.
The House Divided speech,

29
00:02:11,378 --> 00:02:14,658
June 1858: what he said,
where that comes from,

30
00:02:14,708 --> 00:02:19,248
and what it's linked to. The
Dred Scott decision before it,

31
00:02:19,298 --> 00:02:22,138
and after it, the debates
with Stephen Douglas:

32
00:02:22,188 --> 00:02:26,418
seven of them in eight
weeks, from Ottawa to Alton.

33
00:02:26,468 --> 00:02:31,393
He lost that race. Two years
later he was president-elect.

34
00:02:33,033 --> 00:02:36,623
His presidency is a band of
four years and six weeks,

35
00:02:36,673 --> 00:02:40,893
and the war covers almost
all of it. Separate by tags,

36
00:02:40,943 --> 00:02:45,283
choose slavery, and one argument
runs through his whole life:

37
00:02:45,333 --> 00:02:50,583
from a protest he signed in the Illinois
House in 1837 to the amendment that

38
00:02:50,633 --> 00:02:52,186
ended slavery.

39
00:02:53,736 --> 00:03:00,396
Zoom into 1862 and the links give the
order. In July he reads a proclamation to

40
00:03:00,446 --> 00:03:04,366
his cabinet and is told
to wait for a victory.

41
00:03:04,416 --> 00:03:09,486
Antietam comes in September; five days
later, the preliminary proclamation,

42
00:03:09,536 --> 00:03:15,536
and on New Year's Day, the real one.
By August 1864 he expects to lose

43
00:03:15,586 --> 00:03:22,556
re-election, and writes it down. He wins.
And at his second inaugural he asks

44
00:03:22,606 --> 00:03:24,648
for malice toward none.

45
00:03:26,208 --> 00:03:29,858
Five days after Lee surrenders,
he's shot at Ford's Theatre,

46
00:03:29,908 --> 00:03:35,278
at about a quarter past ten at night.
He dies at 7:22 the next morning.

47
00:03:35,328 --> 00:03:40,124
The funeral train takes twelve
days to carry him home.

